# What the API image build must not be handed.
#
# The build context is the repository root, because the image copies the
# application from `src/` and its carrier from `tools/`. Without this file that
# context is roughly 1.9 GiB, nearly all of it the model cache -- 1.71 GiB of
# weights streamed to the daemon on every build, to be used by nothing. The
# `COPY` lines in deploy/api/Dockerfile would still copy only what they name, so
# the resulting image is the same either way; the cost is paid transferring the
# context, which is why an ignore file rather than a narrower Dockerfile is the
# fix.
#
# It is also a boundary. Model weights, virtual environments, kubeconfigs and
# Terraform state have no business being readable by a build, and the surest way
# for one to end up in an image is for it to be in the context when somebody adds
# a broader COPY later.

# Version control and its metadata.
.git/
.gitattributes
.gitignore

# The model cache: 1.71 GiB of weights the API image never reads. The runtime
# reads them from a PersistentVolumeClaim, not from an image layer.
.cache/

# Host state and generated output.
.artifacts/
.venv/
.kube/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Terraform state and plans describe one contributor's cluster.
**/.terraform/
**/*.tfstate
**/*.tfstate.*
**/*.tfplan

# Nothing below is application code, and none of it belongs in a serving image.
tests/
docs/
charts/
infra/
deploy/
scripts/
contracts/
uv.lock
CHANGELOG.md
CONTRIBUTING.md
README.md

# The host tooling the image deliberately does not ship. `tools/api_carrier` and
# `tools/api_container` are the two exceptions and are re-included, because a
# serving container needs its carrier and its entrypoint and nothing else --
# not a model downloader, a container packager, or a certification driver.
tools/*
!tools/__init__.py
!tools/api_carrier/
!tools/api_container/

# Re-exclude bytecode under the two re-included directories. Docker applies these
# rules in order and the last match wins, so the directory re-includes above
# override the `**/__pycache__/` rule for anything inside them -- which would
# ship whatever bytecode the building machine happened to have generated, and
# make the image content, and therefore its digest, differ between two people
# building the same commit. That digest is the thing this whole build path exists
# to make trustworthy, so the exclusion is restated here where it wins.
tools/api_carrier/**/__pycache__/
tools/api_container/**/__pycache__/
tools/api_carrier/**/*.pyc
tools/api_container/**/*.pyc
